Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Test Tone Falls Past Midnight
- Story & Context: After the repair shop closes, Leon leaves an old signal generator connected to a faulty amplifier. Its test tone begins stable, then drifts downward as the unit warms. Mai hears the falling pitch from upstairs and matches it on the đàn bầu, not by changing the fundamental but by bending one harmonic gradually below its expected center. Celeste holds a single minor-9 Rhodes voicing while Leon programs a dry LinnDrum pattern with almost no hi-hat. Elias enters only when the test tone and đàn bầu cross the same pitch, marking the moment with one muted-trumpet note. The track’s central groove begins after the electronic tone is switched off; the band continues reproducing the remembered fall from ear alone. The climax is not a chorus but a clean return to the original unbent harmonic, at which point the entire stereo field contracts for two bars before the bassline resumes.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A small analog test oscillator on a cluttered repair bench after midnight, frequency dial slowly slipping lower, black-lacquer đàn bầu string visible across the foreground, LinnDrum buttons dimly lit, cream paper repair tags, fluorescent reflection on dark tile, realistic cinematic photography. If spoken text is used, limit it to “hold the first tone,” “it is falling,” “same pitch now,” and “switch it off.”

Track 2: Seven Parts, No Chord Name
- Story & Context: During a bend-count rehearsal, Elias identifies a strong upper partial that sits uncomfortably between the notes available on Celeste’s keyboard. Instead of approximating it immediately, the band removes the Rhodes and builds the entire track around the discrepancy. Mai sustains the harmonic and bends through the unstable region repeatedly. Leon writes a two-bar electric-bass figure that avoids the third entirely, leaving the chord identity open. Celeste answers with dry clavinet chops and single vibraphone notes tuned to the nearest stable partials. Elias alternates bass clarinet and muted trumpet, never sounding more than two notes in one phrase. When the Rhodes finally enters near the end, it plays only an open fifth and added second, refusing to name the chord. The title comes from the rehearsal sheet, where the ensemble wrote “partial seven” in place of a harmonic symbol.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Overhead view of a rehearsal table with a handwritten sheet marked only 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, a small tuning meter deliberately set aside, vibraphone mallets, bass-clarinet reed case, muted trumpet cup mute, and đàn bầu bridge visible at frame edge. Warm task-light realism, muted cream, lacquer black, brass, and faded green palette. Spoken fragments limited to “do not name the chord,” “seven is enough,” and “leave the third out.”

Track 3: Clavinet Between Two Bends
- Story & Context: Celeste develops a sixteen-note clavinet figure that sounds too busy whenever Mai plays over it. Rather than simplify the harmony, she removes every note that coincides with the beginning or end of an đàn bầu bend. The remaining fragments produce an irregular but highly danceable rhythm made entirely from the spaces between gestures. Leon mirrors those missing accents with LinnDrum handclaps and a short synth-bass answer, turning absence into the groove’s main organizing principle. Elias plays bass clarinet only on the lowest two notes of each bass phrase and leaves the rest of the measure empty. In the bridge, the rhythm section disappears except for handclaps while Mai performs three increasingly wide pitch bends. Celeste re-enters after each bend with fewer clavinet notes, until the final chorus contains only four dry attacks per bar. The track ends with one unprocessed string pluck and the mechanical click of the clavinet mute release.
